<content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity introduces how to simplify any expression with an exponent of 0. It also introduces the concept that a
  negative exponent is equivalent to the reciprocal of the expression while flipping the sign of the exponent.</para>
<para>As students complete the activity, monitor for those who:</para>
<list>
  <item> try to reduce any value with an exponent of 0 to 0. </item>
  <item> transfer a negative exponent to a negative sign in the base of the exponential expression. </item>
</list>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Review the Zero Exponent Property. Stress that this is true for any value. If needed to reinforce this concept,
  complete the first row in the table as a class.</para>
<para>Limit the time spent on the first table to 5 minutes and pause the class for questions before students move on to the
  second table.</para>
<para>Then have students complete the second table. Read the definition of a negative exponent written in the middle
  column. Complete one row in the table as a class to reinforce comprehension.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>

<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Zero Exponent Property</para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a^0=1\).</para>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a\) to the power of zero equals 1.</para>
  <para>Any non-zero number raised to the zero power is 1.</para>
</note><para>Complete problems 1 - 6 using the product property for exponents.</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(d^0\) </item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(7^0\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer: \(1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((-8)^0\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((-v)^0\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer: \(1\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(r^0 \cdot r^0\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(1\)</para>



<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Definition of a Negative Exponent</para>
    <hr/>
  <para> If \(n\) is an integer and \(a\neq0\), then \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\).</para>
</note><para>Complete problems 6 - 10 using the product property for exponents.</para>


<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(g^{-4}\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(\frac{1}{g^4}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(2^{-3}\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(\frac{1}{2^3}\) or \(\frac18\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\frac{1}{c^{-2}}\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(c^2\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\frac{1}{(-h)^{-6}}\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \((-h)^6\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(\frac{1}{4^{-3}}\)</item>
</list>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold"> Answer: \(4^3\) or \(64\)</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Ask students to summarize the Zero Exponent Property in their own words. Add any missing details as needed. Emphasize
  that any base raised to a power of 0 is always equal to 1. </para>
<para>Ask students to summarize the definition of a negative exponent in their own words. Ask students how they would find
  an equivalent expression using non-negative exponents. If needed, revisit the rule written in the table.</para>
<title> 5.1.3: Self Check </title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold os-raise-text-italicize">After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the
    concepts explored in the activity.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>

<para>Which of the following equations is correct?</para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="2"><thead>
    <row>
      <entry>Answers</entry>
      <entry>Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(8^0=0\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Any non-zero base raised to the power of zero equals 1. The
        answer is \(d^{-4}=\frac{1}{d^4}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(w^{-6}=\frac{-6}{w^6}\)
      </entry><entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The –6 is an exponent, not a factor. Remember that
        \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\), so \(w^{-6}=\frac{1}{w^6}\). The answer is \(d^{-4}=\frac{1}{d^4}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(7^{-3}=-343\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is the result of finding the opposite of \(7^3\).
        Remember that \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\), so \(7^{-3}=\frac{1}{7^3}\). The answer is
        \(d^{-4}=\frac{1}{d^4}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(d^{-4}=\frac{1}{d^4}\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: Remember that \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<title>5.1.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold os-raise-text-italicize">The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with
    the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with
    those who could benefit from it. </para>
<title>Using the Zero Exponent Property</title>
<para>A special case of the Quotient Property is when the exponents of the numerator and denominator are equal, such as an
  expression like \(\frac{a^m}{a^m}\). We know \(\frac{x}{x}=1\) for any \(x\) (\(x\neq0\)) since any number divided by
  itself is 1. The Quotient Property for Exponents shows us how to simplify \(\frac{a^m}{a^m}\) when \(m&gt;n\) and when
  \(n&lt;m\) by subtracting exponents. What if \(m=n\)? We will simplify \(\frac{a^m}{a^m}\) in two ways to lead us to
  the definition of the <emphasis>Zero Exponent Property</emphasis>. In general, for \(a\neq0\):</para>
<media><image alt="" role="presentation" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/568a972a3e26b433291b56a3ca270be5ebdfaa57"/></media><note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Zero Exponent Property </para>
    <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a^0=1\).</para>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a\) to the power of zero equals 1.</para>
  <para>Any non-zero number raised to the zero power is 1.</para>
</note>
<title>Using the Definition of a Negative Exponent</title>
<para>We saw that the Quotient Property for Exponents has two forms depending on whether the exponent is larger in the
  numerator or the denominator. What if we just subtract exponents regardless of which is larger?</para>
<para>Let’s consider \(\frac{x^2}{x^5}\). We subtract the exponent in the denominator from the exponent in the
  numerator. We see \(\frac{x^2}{x^5}\) is \(x^{2-5}\) or \(x^{-3}\).</para>
<para>We can also simplify \(\frac{x^2}{x^5}\) by dividing out common factors:</para>
<para>\(\frac{x^2}{x^5}\)</para>
<para>
  <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
    <mfrac>
      <mrow>
        <menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <mi>x</mi>
        </menclose>
        <mo> </mo>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mo> </mo>
        <menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <mi>x</mi>
        </menclose>
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        <menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <mi>x</mi>
        </menclose>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <menclose notation="updiagonalstrike">
          <mi>x</mi>
        </menclose>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mi>x</mi>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mi>x</mi>
        <mo>·</mo>
        <mi>x</mi>
      </mrow>
    </mfrac>
  </math>
</para>
<para>\(\frac{1}{x^3}\)</para>
<para>This implies that \(x^{-3}=\frac{1}{x^3}\), and it leads us to the definition of a negative exponent. If \(n\) is an
  integer and \(a\neq0\), then \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\).</para>
<para>Let’s now look at what happens to a fraction whose numerator is one and whose denominator is an integer raised
  to a negative exponent.</para>
<para>\(\frac{1}{a^{−n}}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Use the definition of a negative exponent, \(a^{−n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Simplify the complex fraction.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - 
  </emphasis>Multiply.</para>
<para>This implies \(\frac{1}{a^{-n}}=a^n\) and is another form of the definition of <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">Properties of Negative Exponents</term>.</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Properties of Negative Exponents </para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(n\) is an integer and \(a\neq0\), then \(a^{−n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\) or \(\frac{1}{a^{−n}}=a^n\).</para>
</note>
<para>The negative exponent tells us we can rewrite the expression by taking the reciprocal of the base and then changing
  the sign of the exponent.</para>
<para>Any expression that has negative exponents is not considered to be in simplest form. We will use the definition of a
  negative exponent and other properties for exponents to write the expression with only positive exponents.</para>
<para>For example, if after simplifying an expression we end up with the expression \(x^{-3}\), we will take one more step
  and write \(\frac{1}{x^3}\). The answer is considered to be in simplest form when it has only positive exponents.</para>
<title>Try It: Using the Zero Exponent Property and Negative Exponents</title>
<para>Simplify each expression.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(
    h^0\) </item>
  <item> \(7^0\) </item>
  <item>\(
    (-g)^0\) </item>
  <item> \(a^{-4}\) </item>
  <item>\(9^{-3}\) </item>
  <item> \(\frac{1}{n^{-5}}\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to simplify the expressions:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item> 1 </item>
  <item>\(\frac{1}{a^4}\) </item>
  <item>\(\frac{1}{9^3}=\frac{1}{729}\) </item>
  <item> \(n^5\) </item>
</list>
</section>
</content>
